# When did the last country stop selling leaded petrol?
subtitle: "Guess the year the last country stopped selling leaded petrol, then see how long the world took, what it did for children, and who invented it."
tags: ["health","history","chemistry"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"Surely that was ages ago\" note \"Leaded petrol\""

## When did the last country stop selling leaded petrol?
text: {"font_size":34}
sources:
    - id: owid_ban
      title: Every country has now banned the use of leaded gasoline in cars
      authors: Hannah Ritchie (Our World in Data), from UNEP
      year: 2024
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/every-country-has-now-banned-the-use-of-leaded-gasoline-in-cars
      finding: Japan was the first country to ban leaded gasoline in cars, in 1986; Algeria was the last, in 2021
    - id: owid_chart
      title: Global phase-out of leaded petrol in road vehicles
      authors: Our World in Data, from the UN Environment Programme
      year: 2021
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/lead-petrol-ban
      finding: 'bans by year: United States 1996, United Kingdom 2000, Algeria 2021'
    - id: owid_lead
      title: Around one-in-three children globally suffer from lead poisoning. What can we do to reduce this?
      authors: Hannah Ritchie (Our World in Data)
      year: 2022
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/reducing-lead-poisoning
      finding: median blood lead in US children fell by 95% between 1978 and 2016; around one child in three worldwide still has lead poisoning
    - id: midgley
      title: Thomas Midgley Jr.
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Midgley_Jr.
      finding: Midgley found tetraethyllead as a petrol additive in 1921 and developed CFCs (Freon) in the late 1920s

    icon pump size 160 set twemoji x 130 y 580 icon_look picture of "fuel pump" hidden true
    scale yr cites ["owid_ban"] x 90 y 380 width 840 font_size 34 min 1900 max 2030 value 2021 markers [{"value":1986,"label":"Japan","color":"#2f6b8f"},{"value":1996,"label":"USA","color":"#2f6b8f"}] hidden true
    icon flag_dz size 120 set twemoji x 871.8461538461538 y 640 icon_look picture of "flag algeria" hidden true
    text y2021 "Algeria" #b5482e cites ["owid_ban"] x 721.8461538461538 y 640 font_size 52 hidden true
    text y1921 "1921: lead added" #5e584e cites ["midgley"] x 315.6923076923077 y 460 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "When did leaded petrol end?"

A: Petrol with lead in it once filled almost every car on Earth. So when did the last country stop selling it?
    draw pump yr

B: Easy, that's ancient history. Sometime in the eighties, with the shoulder pads?
    highlight yr

    ask "Click on the line where you think it is." on yr store y default "1990" tolerance 0.05 after keep
        right: Right around then. Surprisingly recent, isn't it?
        wrong: No: it was much later than most people think.

A: Much later than the eighties. The last holdout was Algeria, in 2021.
    draw y2021 flag_dz

B: 2021? I was streaming films in 2021, and somebody was still filling up with lead?
    highlight y2021

A: It had started in 1921, when an American chemist found lead stopped engines knocking. Japan banned it first, in 1986, and America ten years later.
    draw y1921 yr_marker_1 yr_marker_2

    erase y2021 flag_dz

B: So thirty-five years from the first ban to the last. Was it worth all that fuss?
    path brace thick #2f6b8f points [[645.6923076923077,540],[645.6923076923077,555],[871.8461538461538,555],[871.8461538461538,540]]
    text span "35 years" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_ban"] x 758.7692307692307 y 590 font_size 48

    erase brace span

A: Very. In American children, lead in the blood fell ninety-five percent from 1978 to 2016. Though worldwide, one child in three still has too much.
    arrow fall #2f6b8f from.x 594 from.y 640 to.x 839.5384615384615 to.y 500 style.stroke_width 5
    text blood "child blood lead −95%" #2f6b8f cites ["owid_lead"] x 330 y 630 font_size 40
    text third "still 1 child in 3" #b5482e cites ["owid_lead"] x 330 y 550 font_size 40

    erase pump yr yr_marker_1 yr_marker_2 yr_answer y1921 fall blood third

A: One more thing about that chemist. His name was Thomas Midgley.
    icon m_pump size 240 set twemoji x 190 y 500 icon_look picture of "fuel pump"
    text m_pump_t "1921: lead" #5e584e cites ["midgley"] x 190 y 355 font_size 46
    icon chemist size 240 set twemoji x 500 y 500 icon_look picture of scientist
    text m_name "Thomas Midgley" #2f2b26 x 500 y 650 font_size 44

B: Don't tell me. He had another big idea.
    highlight chemist

A: He did: CFCs, the gases that later tore a hole in the ozone layer. One historian says no single organism has harmed the atmosphere more.
    icon m_cfc size 240 set twemoji x 810 y 500 or ["aerosol","ice"] icon_look picture of "spray can"
    text m_cfc_t "1920s: CFCs" #b5482e cites ["midgley"] x 810 y 355 font_size 46

B: One man, both disasters. Remind me never to let him near my fridge.
    highlight m_pump_t m_cfc_t

